# 1. Purpose

The Philips Hue Bridge integration enables Aura to discover, pair with, control and monitor Philips Hue systems through the local network.

The integration must make lighting feel like a native part of Aura rather than a separate third-party subsystem.

Users should be able to control individual lights, rooms, zones, scenes, accessories and supported dynamic lighting features without understanding Hue API concepts.

Aura should translate Hue resources into Aura’s unified device, room, capability, scene and diagnostics models.

---

# 2. Product Goals

The Hue integration shall be:

- Local-first
- Low latency
- Reliable
- Secure
- Event-driven
- Capability-aware
- Battery efficient
- Recoverable after network changes
- Scalable to large Hue installations
- Compatible with Aura Scenes and Automations

The user experience should remain calm and simple even when the underlying Hue system contains many bridges, rooms, zones, lights and accessories.

---

# 3. Non-Goals

Version 1 does not aim to:

- Replace the official Philips Hue application
- Recreate advanced bridge administration
- Manage firmware updates directly
- Expose raw Hue API resources to users
- Support cloud-only operation
- Provide professional architectural lighting calibration
- Implement real-time Hue Entertainment streaming in the first release
- Duplicate every manufacturer-specific effect immediately

Unsupported functionality must remain hidden rather than shown as disabled controls.

---

# 4. Supported Hardware

Primary target:

- Philips Hue Bridge v2

Supported resource categories should include, where available:

- Bridge
- Lights
- Rooms
- Zones
- Grouped lights
- Scenes
- Smart plugs
- Motion sensors
- Contact sensors
- Temperature sensors
- Light-level sensors
- Wall switches
- Dimmer switches
- Smart buttons
- Tap Dial devices
- Device batteries
- Entertainment configurations

The plugin must detect actual bridge capabilities at runtime and must not rely only on product-name assumptions.

---

# 5. API Strategy

Aura should prefer the current local Philips Hue API and treat legacy API support as a compatibility layer only where necessary.

The Hue integration is divided into the following layers:

```text
Aura UI
    ↓
Aura Core
    ↓
Unified Capability Model
    ↓
HueBridgePlugin
    ↓
HueRepository
    ↓
HueAPIClient / HueEventStream
    ↓
Local Hue Bridge
```

No SwiftUI view may communicate directly with the bridge.

No Hue-specific response model may leak into the general Aura UI layer.

---

# 6. High-Level Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    UI[Aura UI] --> CORE[Aura Core]
    CORE --> CAP[Unified Capability Model]
    CAP --> PLUGIN[HueBridgePlugin]
    PLUGIN --> REPO[HueRepository]
    REPO --> API[HueAPIClient]
    REPO --> EVENTS[HueEventStream]
    API --> BRIDGE[Hue Bridge]
    EVENTS --> BRIDGE
    REPO --> CACHE[Local Cache]
    PLUGIN --> DIAG[Hue Diagnostics]
```

The plugin must expose stable Aura entities even when the bridge changes IP address or returns resources in a different order.

---

# 7. Core Responsibilities

The HueBridgePlugin shall:

- Discover Hue bridges
- Pair and authenticate
- Store credentials securely
- Load bridge resources
- Map Hue resources to Aura entities
- Read current device state
- Send state-change commands
- Subscribe to bridge events
- Maintain local state consistency
- Support scenes and grouped actions
- Recover after network interruptions
- Report diagnostics
- Expose only supported capabilities

